#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125298} :activity/id #uuid "00d7af21-acdd-52ae-8ffc-37290b0f0c74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$ *\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$ *\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68a96106-e224-5989-af04-8d464b6e58d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35b375f8-a01b-56db-97b7-ac88f2a7883a" :step/math-academy-id 11200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385508}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "74d5fcb5-d32b-5ddd-92cd-491ff00e4b54" :step/math-academy-id 11376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423051}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "147b9868-60b3-5d25-8663-966137fb6540" :step/math-academy-id 11379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "86223826-30df-5eba-9375-355401199095" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2524 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "81eef922-1c17-502c-89a2-3e132f4a64e6" :step/math-academy-id 11383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514463}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"} :step/id #uuid "20b0d4d3-7059-5c01-9a72-d14629d41a1c" :step/math-academy-id 11384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510409}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "266600d8-59f0-586e-ba73-083efb5b7259" :step/math-academy-id 11387}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124731}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406788} :activity/id #uuid "70dc8b50-8f87-5b55-9f2e-265dbd1adbfc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd5310c-dfc2-5a7d-bac5-5825f5064da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406775} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd3692c-18d7-5dde-9e79-ca2c71d664a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} {:answer/id #uuid "cd07008c-2d5e-5372-aa7f-53d2714271f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406778} {:answer/id #uuid "e415ebda-6949-5999-a629-48685dbda1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406781} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a7f1a1-3119-55e9-ab80-51ececd277a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f78fc66-8357-5b97-ab1a-5d79ee0e7c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "077d2dca-c9d8-5043-92cb-18878e5ddc66" :question/math-academy-id "q-71397" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "6c546df4-95bc-53a6-8314-f63e977926a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1d5e01-e81c-5544-a929-055747dc7f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406774} {:answer/id #uuid "82778c6a-7758-50ec-ad08-3720a9d490ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406777} {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc8cce-7237-5eb6-ac41-6a5901ea8c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406780} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf6508f-66f3-52c3-8fbf-477ac88383db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e7ba4e-7d61-5628-8ce5-894dfba410f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef115c0b-3981-5c8f-8e9d-f41c20799c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130719" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}})-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "854e10c9-a2d4-5612-8167-ef459f663827" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406790}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9853cb-1b4d-5d99-b740-f207d47a13c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263384} {:answer/id #uuid "62cb3b0d-c5e0-5140-9b14-0b73eecbc9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263854} {:answer/id #uuid "70e32100-046e-5e0e-b9f2-eac7fe211b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263976} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5900d-9aee-5b56-aa97-4e95d9c8f10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} :answer-field/id #uuid "263638a6-9832-58e0-a5f7-da0d13d29e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d42d4bf9-4eef-57b6-b4ed-d5448bfe2b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130723" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9536b8a9-959c-5b6e-8ff0-53d077d7ad52"}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406773}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423055} :activity/id #uuid "4c8248e9-9c46-5103-ac9c-f0a394655226" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed1a1ab0-6d18-5f30-b80b-be9f46dd80b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398363} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cbc8d4-c2ee-5478-860e-c731a5274aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398364} {:answer/id #uuid "f499f957-93d0-5ca6-8e65-57c931e33b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aa6514-a3ad-578c-8808-8284836b7b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398366} {:answer/id #uuid "431e58b2-3a4b-54f5-a2fc-05d378bf57ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} :answer-field/id #uuid "530f90fa-b927-569c-b4dd-79831f2442af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da3be12b-b270-5258-9b1c-409842422d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-131116" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "1470ea07-3b96-5965-8beb-c2e0041edd4a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd12d1d6-f236-57c3-bd6d-b0a476bef5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373141} {:answer/id #uuid "e0818182-cafe-552e-a4a0-98b0c1314881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z+1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373142} {:answer/id #uuid "df5c957f-327c-59b5-a275-854f61c6c1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+1=y-1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373143} {:answer/id #uuid "4643467a-d570-58d2-ac66-45f1f209b1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373144} {:answer/id #uuid "c777726a-c485-5a89-bead-338bff4761cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373145} :answer-field/id #uuid "31f112b8-6ada-53ff-b6b3-be00d57b76b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d41ffa1d-b743-559f-b433-9851a75bb790" :question/math-academy-id "q-71408" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ at the point $(-1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1,1) & =2(-1)\\text{ }i+2(-1)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-2\\,i-2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20ee70ca-88be-504d-b7aa-237d8d22e58a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364ddf2b-cdd1-5e21-88c2-478a7d637e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423045} {:answer/id #uuid "415a3975-289f-5c0a-aa48-bc85b813f82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423046} {:answer/id #uuid "6321cb0e-8e68-5a7b-9a36-ac871cc17b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423047} {:answer/id #uuid "82ce7cc2-be21-5dc4-9b04-e941ddd03d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} {:answer/id #uuid "ba63a292-9d83-540d-ac68-00ce4812ebb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} :answer-field/id #uuid "02401848-522e-501b-82ff-6e5cb3f48ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f16c0b1-b80b-5067-adaf-a65b04fb7bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130721" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${e}^{{x}^{2}y}={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}y} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9 \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}+3{x}^{2})i+({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-2y)j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =(2(2)(0){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}+3({2}^{2}))i+(({2}^{2}){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}-2(0))j \\\\  & =12\\,i+4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨12,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨12,4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{12} & =\\frac{y-0}{4} \\\\ 4(x-2) & =12y \\\\ x-2 & =3y \\\\ x-3y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "68e1c4ea-40a6-5a21-ae75-071da89109f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dd151b9-79f9-5a66-8557-365b16a9535a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373008} {:answer/id #uuid "e407b4cd-b0e3-5ef2-a3e1-1c2c4a208e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{y}{2}=-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373009} {:answer/id #uuid "2042c239-c608-579c-a356-780f349dc015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{2}=y+1=-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373010} {:answer/id #uuid "e11362e5-c68a-5d93-8627-d0fe7c1b9816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373011} {:answer/id #uuid "dad753bd-efc6-5cc5-bf02-04d7fe01e2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373010} :answer-field/id #uuid "f074ec94-f4ab-59e4-98e4-6833b7d43d47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a1e0fe3a-1bd3-5ee4-ab1a-74e46b29c2c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36351" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)$ at the point $(1,-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z$, then our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,0).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2x+y}\\text{ }j-1\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }j-k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=y+1=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6fe264a7-c045-5612-8184-b7672b802421" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68112ca-e7ee-5f12-8c6b-6f964b2fce64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398915} {:answer/id #uuid "5739b0cd-fba7-5818-952f-616d15f8cd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\,i-4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398916} {:answer/id #uuid "fc61dd7b-3fa9-5347-ad13-eae563990528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398917} {:answer/id #uuid "9e270cd2-2ed5-5ff9-a8d2-046b4683fe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,i+4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398918} {:answer/id #uuid "12f59294-d897-5332-9fd4-6a0a58840be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-2j+6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398916} :answer-field/id #uuid "027e0dd0-8fb0-53bb-95b9-27c1e0e20a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15fe86f3-62bf-5e0d-8567-00ad11df9f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65167" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-2yz+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(2xy-2z)\\text{ }j+(-2y+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+(2(1)(-1)-2(1))\\,j+(-2(-1)+1)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i-4j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "80a30235-ea9e-5be6-ade1-4b81b30de9fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423053}}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423044}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497419} :activity/id #uuid "5705edea-b336-51ef-88f9-19f848c1f228" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-5y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496664} {:answer/id #uuid "132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x-3y+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496700} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496894} {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5x-4y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b9919e-c058-55c4-a73e-14ca053dbdce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb219d72-040d-55dd-90ca-3f831acb4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65173" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "053dd984-66f7-5e60-a777-6ca3631e9fa4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497423}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} {:answer/id #uuid "8070bb66-d16c-505c-85ee-2a417bc81d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398957} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdcaf70-4358-5973-a594-026557c83288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398958} {:answer/id #uuid "61e68fe8-0332-53d0-b214-d38a39b52899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398959} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c9c882-083f-5978-8947-be71bba2f10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} :answer-field/id #uuid "91095769-6695-5735-bdf7-d8d4bfdca38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58a67c4a-aa8b-5b64-8268-56928e2716de" :question/math-academy-id "q-71399" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "14aa383b-ac4e-5a2e-b93c-5310c25c9ba2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076ccae2-d102-5ad8-b1c2-ac28978e3e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263068} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3882de-2108-52bf-b5e1-4c3304b9a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a0770-0459-500c-aa32-352ae1667e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264813} {:answer/id #uuid "d493ac59-d9ae-5896-bd9e-006d111d56b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7e101e-fe81-5db4-a578-c892744685d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c80428ab-daca-506e-aca7-d4e40b0d604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "17889977-02c9-5363-abaf-c90dc6ba9c00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497443}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4345e16b-0df8-59b8-ad37-650db4ac210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263593} {:answer/id #uuid "58520017-6d75-5711-9c4c-279ff100c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263761} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8b5e1d-96ce-502e-ac9c-14b8cdee3daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264220} {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} {:answer/id #uuid "e315f8f8-9238-56c6-b08f-3a589b1dff97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90461b1-1149-54d3-9d43-9942adac1495" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b9d3e0c-64e0-51a4-97be-e5302f43c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131126" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "648fbdd8-b44b-5888-8db1-2712803cf64d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367ab4f2-93bc-5938-9462-dc0e018dd590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496776} {:answer/id #uuid "399e3797-afa8-52bc-9d91-1f755a50f199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496783} {:answer/id #uuid "d59ec8f6-4eba-53b8-a3b8-5a5f6ce7e72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497104} {:answer/id #uuid "db86fc5b-7216-54fe-8379-494c5e62c9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497123} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497200} :answer-field/id #uuid "631be222-c76c-5ee5-9b25-874887015acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca2288f3-367d-50c0-9754-21f3665fdec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71406" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1))\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "bf1f0a54-a2e4-5d5b-9366-b7874e2b2325" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497425}}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496648}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514514} :activity/id #uuid "f14a258c-8230-5ba0-9c10-e3821900b845" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201fe908-79f1-5c96-8882-603760dea14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513619} {:answer/id #uuid "3f49c5b4-7cf8-5c1b-9bb1-15cfaacad0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513700} {:answer/id #uuid "45b310bb-a17f-5c57-88ef-88eaeb7ca1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513719} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb3285b-1658-5c85-a2b4-99659e2beceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513831} {:answer/id #uuid "92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e47ae0-7d67-5cf0-9030-1e73b5fe057a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" :question/math-academy-id "q-71405" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,k \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz))\\text{ }j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(-(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0))\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$"} :step/id #uuid "2c774462-5887-556e-8c1c-42d4884f6cfe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-5y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496664} {:answer/id #uuid "132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x-3y+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496700} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496894} {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5x-4y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b9919e-c058-55c4-a73e-14ca053dbdce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb219d72-040d-55dd-90ca-3f831acb4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65173" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a1f6e0c-4f5f-50c7-b0d9-d452532aced4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00dfea87-0564-54b8-b2ba-b85b9b81f898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263009} {:answer/id #uuid "02bdc94f-aecb-598c-8c8e-fd84d09e16d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1=y-2=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263024} {:answer/id #uuid "2143561b-a016-53c4-935f-4521d8cb87c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{y-2}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263298} {:answer/id #uuid "71e1bc5e-929a-5149-b69e-6390e2a09556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263985} {:answer/id #uuid "cd91307b-95b0-5c36-965f-60e3ce8f9695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8f2342-92e1-5c88-9098-f4db5aaee8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46c7ac33-5b38-5319-85fa-4f19aa20d07b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131130" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $x={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(3,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a9a38a9f-1171-55bf-a06a-39b676731e34"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17754529-babb-5cf0-8d95-1b9cc9537939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336252} {:answer/id #uuid "2027b715-6614-5f66-82b2-1f71af6cb775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+\\frac{1}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336332} {:answer/id #uuid "36a67130-4f88-5f7d-9e66-e328e09a4789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336538} {:answer/id #uuid "530705a9-532d-591f-bbf7-492c24eb6728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336798} {:answer/id #uuid "8a797477-b113-50ff-8e91-509061d9a654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280} :answer-field/id #uuid "380e5863-1f99-5c29-acf5-cfab37a637d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7aa18077-2806-56e2-99dd-f7365f248356" :question/math-academy-id "q-71403" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "d0f5d1fa-3cf9-5f94-8815-62bf015679e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076ccae2-d102-5ad8-b1c2-ac28978e3e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263068} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3882de-2108-52bf-b5e1-4c3304b9a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a0770-0459-500c-aa32-352ae1667e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264813} {:answer/id #uuid "d493ac59-d9ae-5896-bd9e-006d111d56b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7e101e-fe81-5db4-a578-c892744685d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c80428ab-daca-506e-aca7-d4e40b0d604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9ae40c4-6ef1-5389-b6b7-49816af3ef18" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514523}}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513524}]}
